        text: `<b>A. "Ban AI from schools — before it's too late" (by T. Morgan)</b>
Everyone knows that students today can't concentrate. Since AI chatbots appeared, homework has become meaningless: a teenager can produce a perfect essay in thirty seconds. When I was at school, we learned by struggling, and we turned out fine. Some tech companies claim AI can personalize learning, but of course they would say that — they want to sell their products. The choice is simple: either we ban AI from classrooms now, or we will raise a generation that cannot think for itself.

<b>B. "Teach with AI, not against it" (by L. Chen)</b>
AI tools are already part of most workplaces, so schools have a responsibility to teach students to use them critically. In a small pilot I observed at two schools, students who used an AI tutor for math practice, with teacher supervision, asked more questions in class than before, although the sample was too small to draw firm conclusions. Banning AI is unlikely to stop students from using it at home; it would only remove adults from the conversation. A better approach would be to redesign assessment — more oral exams, in-class writing and projects — so that learning cannot be outsourced. AI is not a replacement for teachers, but in the hands of good teachers it may become a powerful tool.`,
