        model: "Our parks are not a luxury\n\nWhen was the last time you sat under a tree in our city without hearing traffic? For many residents, the answer is \"never\". As a pediatrician who has worked in the east district for fifteen years, I see the consequences every week: children with nowhere safe to play, parents with nowhere to breathe.\n\nThe council is considering selling the old Riverside lot to developers. The money would help this year's budget. But I ask you: what is the real cost? Researchers in many countries have linked access to green space with better mental and physical health. These benefits do not appear in a budget spreadsheet, but they appear in my clinic.\n\nLet me tell you about Sofía, nine years old. Her family lives in a small apartment by the highway. When a volunteer group opened a temporary garden on the lot last summer, she spent every afternoon there. Her mother told me she slept better, laughed more and stopped asking for her tablet.\n\nSome argue that the city cannot afford a new park. I understand that concern. But a park can be built in phases, with community volunteers and state grants. A building gives us income for a few years. A park gives us health for generations.\n\nGreen space is not a luxury. It is a playground, a meeting place and a medicine without a prescription. I urge readers to attend the council meeting on May 12th and say yes to Riverside Park.",
