      model: "Should AI tools be allowed in schools?\n\nFew educational questions have provoked such heated debate as the role of artificial intelligence in the classroom. While some commentators call for an outright ban, others see AI as an unprecedented opportunity. In my view, schools should allow AI, but only within a framework that protects genuine learning.\n\nThe case for a ban rests largely on the assumption that students will inevitably use AI to avoid thinking. This concern is understandable: if an essay can be produced in seconds, traditional homework loses much of its value. However, arguments that \"everyone knows students cannot concentrate any more\" are hasty generalizations, and presenting the issue as a choice between a total ban and intellectual decline is a false dilemma.\n\nSupporters of AI make a stronger case when they acknowledge its limits. Early classroom pilots appear to suggest that supervised use of AI tutors may encourage some students to ask more questions, although the evidence is still too limited to draw firm conclusions. More convincingly, banning AI at school is unlikely to prevent its use at home; it would merely remove teachers from the conversation.\n\nThe solution, therefore, lies not in prohibition but in redesigning assessment. Oral examinations, in-class writing and project work make learning far harder to outsource, while explicit lessons on how AI works can develop the critical skills students will need at work.\n\nUltimately, AI is neither a miracle nor a menace. It is a tool. Used carelessly, it can replace thinking; used wisely, it can extend it. Schools cannot choose whether their students will live alongside AI. They can only choose whether those students will learn to question it.",
